{
  "retrieved_at": "2026-11-08T04:36:17.940Z",
  "tool": "web.run open",
  "result": "Experimental determination of the solubility constant of kurnakovite, MgB3O3(OH)5⸱5H2O (https://www.rruff.net/odr/view/downloadfile/84029)\nciteturn53view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.rruff.net/odr/view/downloadfile/84029\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 887\nL0@P0: Experimental determination of the solubility constant of kurnakovite, MgB3O3(OH)5⸱5H2O\nL1@P0: Yongliang Xiong1,*,†\nL2@P0: 1\nL3@P0: Nuclear Waste Disposal Research & Analysis, Sandia National Laboratories, 1515 Eubank Boulevard SE, Albuquerque, New Mexico 87123,\nL4@P0: U.S.A.\nL5@P0: Abstract\nL6@P0: In this study, I present experimental results on the equilibrium between boracite [Mg3B7O13Cl(cr)] and\nL7@P0: kurnakovite [chemical formula, Mg2B6O11⸱15H2O(cr); structural formula, MgB3O3(OH)5⸱5H2O(cr)] at\nL8@P0: 22.5 ± 0.5 °C from a long-term experiment up to 1629 days, approaching equilibrium from the direction\nL9@P0: of supersaturation, Mg3B7O13Cl(cr) + H+ + 2B(OH)4\nL10@P0: –\nL11@P0:  + 18H2O(1) ⇌ 3MgB3O3(OH)5⸱5H2O(cr) + Cl–.\nL12@P0: Based on solubility measurements, the 10-based logarithm of the equilibrium constant for the above\nL13@P0: reaction at 25 °C is determined to be 12.83 ± 0.08 (2s).\nL14@P0: Based on the equilibrium constant for dissolution of boracite, Mg3B7O13Cl(cr) + 15H2O(l) = 3Mg2+\nL15@P0: + 7B(OH)4\nL16@P0: –\nL17@P0:  + Cl– + 2H+ at 25 °C measured previously (Xiong et al. 2018) and that for the reaction\nL18@P0: between boracite and kurnakovite determined here, the equilibrium constant for dissolution of kurna\u0002kovite, MgB3O3(OH)5⸱5H2O(cr) = Mg2+ + 3B(OH)4\nL19@P0: –\nL20@P0:  + H+ + H2O(1) is derived as –14.11 ± 0.40 (2s).\nL21@P0: Using the equilibrium constant for dissolution of kurnakovite obtained in this study and the experi\u0002mental enthalpy of formation for kurnakovite from the literature, a set of thermodynamic properties\nL22@P0: for kurnakovite at 25 °C and 1 bar is recommended as follows: ΔH0\nL23@P0: f = –4813.24 ± 4.92 kJ/mol, ΔG0f =\nL24@P0: –4232.0 ± 2.3 kJ/mol, and S0 = 414.3 ± 0.9 J/(mol⸱K). Among them, the Gibbs energy of formation is\nL25@P0: based on the equilibrium constant for kurnakovite determined in this study; the enthalpy of formation\nL26@P0: is from the literature (Li et al. 1997), and the standard entropy is calculated internally with the Gibbs\u0002Helmholtz equation in this work. The thermodynamic properties of kurnakovite estimated using the\nL27@P0: group contribution method for borate minerals based on the sums of contributions from the cations,\nL28@P0: borate polyanions, and structural water to the thermodynamic properties from the literature (Li et al.\nL29@P0: 2000) are consistent, within their uncertainties, with the values listed above.\nL30@P0: Since kurnakovite usually forms in salt lakes rich in sulfate, studying the interactions of borate\nL31@P0: with sulfate is important to modeling kurnakovite in salt lakes. For this purpose, I have re-calibrated\nL32@P0: our previous model (Xiong et al. 2013) describing the interactions of borate with sulfate based on the\nL33@P0: new solubility data for borax in Na2SO4 solutions presented here.\nL34@P0: Keywords: Pitzer model, nuclear waste management, borate deposits, actinide-borate aqueous\nL35@P0: complex, borate minerals, salt lakes, hydrofracking materials, solution chemistry; Lithium, Beryllium\nL36@P0: and Boron: Quintessentially Crustal\nL37@P0: Introduction\nL38@P0: Kurnakovite, which has a structural formula of MgB3O3(OH)5⸱\nL39@P0: 5H2O(cr) (“cr” refers to “crystalline,” thereafter), is a magnesium\nL40@P0: borate mineral with triclinic symmetry in the space group of P1.\nL41@P0: In kurnakovite, there are the following structural units: triangu\u0002larly coordinated [BO2(OH)] groups, tetrahedrally coordinated\nL42@P0: [BO2(OH)2] groups, and Mg(OH)2(H2O)4 octahedra, connected\nL43@P0: into neutral Mg(H2O)4B3O3(OH)5 units forming chains along the\nL44@P0: [001] direction (Gatta et al. 2019). The mineral that has the same\nL45@P0: chemical composition but is of monoclinic symmetry is called\nL46@P0: inderite. Kurnakovite was discovered by Godlevsky (1940) in\nL47@P0: the borate deposits of west Kazakhstan. Rumanova and Ashirov\nL48@P0: (1964) determined its densities and refractive indices. Then, Yeh\nL49@P0: (1965) did structure determinations for kurnakovite. Razmanova\nL50@P0: et al. (1970) solved its structure, and Corazza (1974) made a\nL51@P0: refinement for the structure. Zhou et al. (2012a) refined the\nL52@P0: hydrogen positions using nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR).\nL53@P0: Most recently, Gatta et al. (2019) studied the crystal structure\nL54@P0: of a natural kurnakovite from Kramer Deposit, California, using\nL55@P0: single-crystal neutron diffraction. Kurnakovite was synthesized\nL56@P0: by Spiryagina (1949) and Yuan et al. (1993). Heinrich (1946)\nL57@P0: also synthesized kurnakovite, corresponding to the occurrence\nL58@P0: in the U.S.A., but it seems that he mislabeled it as inderite, as\nL59@P0: Frondel et al. (1956) did (Schaller and Mrose 1960) (see below).\nL60@P0: The currently available thermodynamic properties of kurna\u0002kovite are inconsistent. Li et al. (1997) measured its enthalpy\nL61@P0: of formation, and Li et al. (2000) estimated its Gibbs energy\nL62@P0: of formation to be –4249.79 kJ/mol. By contrast, Anovitz and\nL63@P0: Hemingway (2002) estimated its Gibbs energy of formation to be\nL64@P0: –4272 kJ/mol, differing the estimated value from Li et al. (2000)\nL65@P0: by ~23 kJ/mol. Therefore, its Gibbs free energy of formation is\nL66@P0: not well known, leading to the inexact knowledge of its solubil\u0002American Mineralogist, Volume 105, pages 977–983, 2020\nL67@P0: 0003-004X/20/0007–977$05.00/DOI: https://doi.org/10.2138/am-2020-7212 977\nL68@P0: * E-mail: yxiong@sandia.gov. Orcid 0000-0002-7149-068X.\nL69@P0: † Special collection papers can be found online at http://www.minsocam.org/MSA/\nL70@P0: AmMin/special-collections.html.\nL71@P0: Downloaded from http://pubs.geoscienceworld.org/msa/ammin/article-pdf/105/7/977/5075113/am-2020-7212.pdf\nL72@P0: by University of Arizona user\nL73@P0-1: on 22 September 2020978 XIONG: SOLUBILITY CONSTANT OF KURNAKOVITE\nL74@P1: American Mineralogist, vol. 105, 2020\nL75@P1: ity constant. In this work, the solubility constant of kurnakovite\nL76@P1: is determined to provide a set of its thermodynamic properties.\nL77@P1: Given the occurrence of kurnakovite in sulfate-enriched salt\nL78@P1: lakes in nature, Xiong et al. (2013) developed a model to describe\nL79@P1: the interactions between sulfate and borate based on literature\nL80@P1: data (Sborgi et al. 1924) on the solubility of sodium tetraborate\nL81@P1: (borax) in Na2SO4 solutions without any pH measurements. In\nL82@P1: the present work, the previous model has been recalibrated to\nL83@P1: describe the interactions between sulfate and borate for modeling\nL84@P1: the formation of kurnakovite in sulfate-enriched salt lakes, based\nL85@P1: on our own borax solubility data together with pH measurements\nL86@P1: in Na2SO4 solutions up to 1.8 mol/kgat 25 °C.\nL87@P1: Occurrence of kurnakovite\nL88@P1: Kurnakovite has been reported from about 12 localities world\u0002wide, including the type locality in the gypsum cap of the Inder\nL89@P1: salt dome, Kazakhstan. In the U.S.A., it is present in the Jennifer\nL90@P1: Mine of the Kramer borate deposit, Kern County (Frondel et al.\nL91@P1: 1956; Schaller and Mrose 1960; Pemberton 1983) (however,\nL92@P1: Frondel et al. mislabeled kurnakovite as inderite, according to\nL93@P1: Schaller and Mrose 1960 and Baysal 1973), and in the Furnace\nL94@P1: Creek district, Inyo County (Erd et al. 1970), California. In Turkey,\nL95@P1: it is present in the Kirka borate deposit (Baysal 1973; Inan et al.\nL96@P1: 1973; Helvaci 1978). In China, it occurs in Da Qaidam Lake and\nL97@P1: Xiao Qaidam Lake (Qian and Xuan 1985; Zheng et al. 2005; Li\nL98@P1: et al. 2012, 2013), the Yashatu deposit (Jiang et al. 1996), Qinghai\nL99@P1: Province; in Zhacang Chaka Salt Lake (Zheng and Liu 1982;\nL100@P1: Yang and Zheng 1985), Nie’er Cuo Salt Lake (Yang and Zheng\nL101@P1: 1985; Liu and Zheng 2010), Chala Ka Salt Lake (Yang and Zheng\nL102@P1: 1985), Qag Caca Salt Lake (Yang 1991), and Jibu Salt Lake (Li\nL103@P1: et al. 2004), Xizang (Tibet) Autonomous Region. In Argentina, it\nL104@P1: occurs in the Tincalayu borax mine, Salta (Hurlbut and Erd 1974).\nL105@P1: At several of these localities, kurnakovite commonly forms\nL106@P1: in current salt lakes that are rich in sulfate (Sun et al. 1984; Yang\nL107@P1: 1989; Yuan et al. 1993; Liu et al. 2003; Zheng and Liu 2009),\nL108@P1: and formed in ancient or extinct salt lakes that were also rich in\nL109@P1: sulfate such as Neogene salt lakes in western Turkey (Kistler and\nL110@P1: Helvacı 1994) and Miocene-Pliocene salt lakes in Death Valley,\nL111@P1: California, U.S.A. (Erd et al. 1970; Tanner 2002), as evidenced\nL112@P1: by the occurrence of both kurnakovite and gypsum in Neogene\nL113@P1: and Miocene-Pliocene deposits such as the Kirka borate deposit\nL114@P1: (Helvaci et al. 1993) and the borate deposits in the Furnace Creek\nL115@P1: Formation in Death Valley (Erd et al. 1970; Tanner 2002).\nL116@P1: Applications of kurnakovite\nL117@P1: Kurnakovite has several practical applications. In the oil and\nL118@P1: gas industry, kurnakovite is one of the borate minerals used in\nL119@P1: the hydrofracturing fluids as cross-linkers for extraction of shale\nL120@P1: gas (Dobson et al. 2005). This usage is based on the fact that\nL121@P1: kurnakovite is a sparingly soluble borate mineral. Kurnakovite\nL122@P1: is used for neutron shielding (Senberber et al. 2017; Gatta et al.\nL123@P1: 2019), as kurnakovite has the lowest neutron radiation perme\u0002ability among the common borate minerals (Derun and Kipcak\nL124@P1: 2011). Kurnakovite also has interesting luminescence properties\nL125@P1: with its excitation spectrum at 360 nm and emission spectrum\nL126@P1: at 420 nm (Yuan et al. 1993). In the field of nuclear waste\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRedetermination of the borax structure from laboratory X-ray data at 145 K (https://journals.iucr.org/e/issues/2008/05/00/wm2174/wm2174.pdf)\nciteturn53view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://journals.iucr.org/e/issues/2008/05/00/wm2174/wm2174.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 384\nL0@P0: Redetermination of the borax structure\nL1@P0: from laboratory X-ray data at 145 K\nL2@P0: Graeme J. Gainsford,* Tim Kemmitt and Caleb Higham\nL3@P0: Industrial Research Limited, PO Box 31-310, Lower Hutt, New Zealand\nL4@P0: Correspondence e-mail: g.gainsford@irl.cri.nz\nL5@P0: Received 26 March 2008; accepted 15 April 2008\nL6@P0: Key indicators: single-crystal X-ray study; T = 145 K; mean \u0001(O–B) = 0.001 A˚;\nL7@P0: R factor = 0.025; wR factor = 0.077; data-to-parameter ratio = 15.5.\nL8@P0: The title compound, sodium tetraborate decahydrate (mineral\nL9@P0: name: borax), Na2[B4O5(OH)4]\u00018H2O, has been studied\nL10@P0: previously using X-ray [Morimoto (1956). Miner. J. 2, 1–18]\nL11@P0: and neutron [Levy & Lisensky (1978). Acta Cryst. B34, 3502–\nL12@P0: 3510] diffraction data. The structure contains tetraborate\nL13@P0: anions [B4O5(OH)4]\nL14@P0: 2\u0002 with twofold rotation symmetry, which\nL15@P0: form hydrogen-bonded chains, and [Na(H2O)6] octahedra that\nL16@P0: form zigzag chains [Na(H2O)4/2(H2O)2/1]. The O—H bond\nL17@P0: distances obtained from the present redetermination at 145 K\nL18@P0: are shorter than those in the neutron study by an average of\nL19@P0: 0.127 (19) A˚ .\nL20@P0: Related literature\nL21@P0: For previous studies of the borax structure, see: Morimoto\nL22@P0: (1956); Levy & Lisenky (1978). For other structures listed in\nL23@P0: the Cambridge Structural Database (Allen, 2002) that contain\nL24@P0: the [B4O5(OH)4]\nL25@P0: 2\u0002 anion, see: Wang et al. (2004); Pan et al.\nL26@P0: (2007). For related structures, see: Yi et al. (2005). For\nL27@P0: comparative studies of hydrogen bonds obtained from X-ray\nL28@P0: and neutron data, see: Allen (1986); Smrcˇok et al. (2006).\nL29@P0: Experimental\nL30@P0: Crystal data\nL31@P0: Na2[B4O5(OH)4]\u00018H2O\nL32@P0: Mr = 381.38\nL33@P0: Monoclinic, C2=c\nL34@P0: a = 11.8843 (5) A˚\nL35@P0: b = 10.6026 (4) A˚\nL36@P0: c = 12.2111 (5) A˚\nL37@P0: \u0002 = 106.790 (2)\u0003\nL38@P0: V = 1473.06 (10) A˚ 3\nL39@P0: Z =4\nL40@P0: Mo K\u0003 radiation\nL41@P0: \u0004 = 0.22 mm\u00021\nL42@P0: T = 145 (2) K\nL43@P0: 0.65 \u0004 0.36 \u0004 0.26 mm\nL44@P0: Data collection\nL45@P0: Bruker–Nonius APEX2 CCD area\u0002detector diffractometer\nL46@P0: Absorption correction: multi-scan\nL47@P0: (SADABS; Bruker, 2006)\nL48@P0: Tmin = 0.813, Tmax = 0.94\nL49@P0: 8429 measured reflections\nL50@P0: 2275 independent reflections\nL51@P0: 2137 reflections with I >2\u0001(I)\nL52@P0: Rint = 0.018\nL53@P0: Refinement\nL54@P0: R[F2 >2\u0001(F2)] = 0.025\nL55@P0: wR(F2\nL56@P0: ) = 0.076\nL57@P0: S = 1.08\nL58@P0: 2275 reflections\nL59@P0: 147 parameters\nL60@P0: All H-atom parameters refined\nL61@P0: \u0001\u0005max = 0.37 e A˚ \u00023\nL62@P0: \u0001\u0005min = \u00020.22 e A˚ \u00023\nL63@P0: Table 1\nL64@P0: Selected bond lengths (A˚ ).\nL65@P0: Na1—O8i 2.3815 (6)\nL66@P0: Na1—O6ii 2.3979 (5)\nL67@P0: Na1—O7 2.4121 (6)\nL68@P0: Na2—O7iii 2.4041 (6)\nL69@P0: Na2—O9 2.4214 (6)\nL70@P0: Na2—O6 2.4441 (6)\nL71@P0: B1—O4 1.4451 (8)\nL72@P0: B1—O1 1.4657 (7)\nL73@P0: B1—O2 1.4902 (8)\nL74@P0: B1—O3 1.5075 (8)\nL75@P0: B2—O2 1.3655 (8)\nL76@P0: B2—O3i 1.3757 (8)\nL77@P0: B2—O5 1.3784 (8)\nL78@P0: Symmetry codes: (i) \u0002x; y; \u0002z þ 1\nL79@P0: 2; (ii) \u0002x; y \u0002 1; \u0002z þ 12; (iii) \u0002x; y þ 1; \u0002z þ 12.\nL80@P0: Table 2\nL81@P0: Hydrogen-bond geometry (A˚ , \u0003).\nL82@P0: D—H\u0001\u0001\u0001AD—H H\u0001\u0001\u0001AD\u0001\u0001\u0001AD—H\u0001\u0001\u0001A\nL83@P0: O5—H5iv\u0001\u0001\u0001O3iv 0.836 (15) 1.895 (15) 2.7300 (7) 176.3 (15)\nL84@P0: O4v\nL85@P0: —H4\u0001\u0001\u0001O9vi 0.828 (14) 2.049 (14) 2.8658 (8) 168.4 (12)\nL86@P0: O6—H6Avii\u0001\u0001\u0001O5viii 0.868 (16) 1.978 (16) 2.8323 (8) 167.9 (14)\nL87@P0: O6ix—H6B\u0001\u0001\u0001O4v 0.846 (16) 2.040 (15) 2.8624 (8) 163.9 (16)\nL88@P0: O7vii—H7A\u0001\u0001\u0001O2 0.827 (16) 1.989 (16) 2.8135 (8) 174.1 (12)\nL89@P0: O7—H7B\u0001\u0001\u0001O4 0.816 (15) 2.135 (15) 2.9233 (8) 162.3 (14)\nL90@P0: O8v—H8A\u0001\u0001\u0001O1x 0.866 (13) 1.936 (13) 2.7865 (6) 167.0 (14)\nL91@P0: O8v\nL92@P0: —H8B\u0001\u0001\u0001O5xi 0.855 (15) 2.341 (14) 3.1320 (8) 154.2 (12)\nL93@P0: O9—H9Avii\u0001\u0001\u0001O3 0.843 (16) 2.253 (16) 3.0894 (8) 171.7 (15)\nL94@P0: O9—H9Bxii\u0001\u0001\u0001O8xiii 0.849 (17) 2.069 (16) 2.9034 (8) 167.4 (15)\nL95@P0: Symmetry codes: (iv) x; \u0002y þ 1; z þ 1\nL96@P0: 2; (v) \u0002x þ 1; y; \u0002z þ 12; (vi) x þ 12; y \u0002 12; z; (vii)\nL97@P0: \u0002x þ 1\nL98@P0: 2; y þ 12; \u0002z þ 12; (viii) \u0002x þ 12; \u0002y þ 32; \u0002z þ 1; (ix) \u0002x þ 1; \u0002y þ 1; \u0002z þ 1; (x)\nL99@P0: x þ 1; y; z; (xi) x þ 1\nL100@P0: 2; \u0002y þ 12; z \u0002 12; (xii) x \u0002 12; y þ 12; z; (xiii) x; \u0002y þ 1; z \u0002 12.\nL101@P0: Data collection: APEX2 (Bruker, 2006); cell refinement: APEX2;\nL102@P0: data reduction: APEX2; program(s) used to solve structure:\nL103@P0: SHELXS97 (Sheldrick, 2008); program(s) used to refine structure:\nL104@P0: SHELXL97 (Sheldrick, 2008); molecular graphics: ORTEP-3\nL105@P0: (Farrugia, 1997) and Mercury (Macrae et al., 2006); software used to\nL106@P0: prepare material for publication: SHELXL97 and PLATON (Spek,\nL107@P0: 2003).\nL108@P0: We thank Dr J. Wikaira of the University of Canterbury,\nL109@P0: New Zealand, for her assistance with the data collection.\nL110@P0: Supplementary data and figures for this paper are available from the\nL111@P0: IUCr electronic archives (Reference: WM2174).\nL112@P0: References\nL113@P0: Allen, F. H. (1986). Acta Cryst. B42, 515–522.\nL114@P0: Allen, F. H. (2002). Acta Cryst. B58, 380–388.\nL115@P0: Bruker (2006). APEX2 and SADABS. Bruker AXS Inc., Madison, Wisconsin,\nL116@P0: USA.\nL117@P0: Farrugia, L. J. (1997). J. Appl. Cryst. 30, 565.\nL118@P0: Levy, H. A. & Lisensky, G. C. (1978). Acta Cryst. B34, 3502–3510.\nL119@P0: Macrae, C. F., Edgington, P. R., McCabe, P., Pidcock, E., Shields, G. P., Taylor,\nL120@P0: R., Towler, M. & van de Streek, J. (2006). J. Appl. Cryst. 39, 453–457.\nL121@P0: Morimoto, N. (1956). Miner. J. 2, 1—18.\nL122@P0: Pan, C.-Y., Wang, G.-M., Zheng, S.-T. & Yang, G.-Y. (2007). Acta Cryst. E63,\nL123@P0: o1207–o1209.\nL124@P0: Sheldrick, G. M. (2008). Acta Cryst. A64, 112–122.\nL125@P0: inorganic compounds\nL126@P0: i24 Gainsford et al. doi:10.1107/S1600536808010441 Acta Cryst. (2008). E64, i24–i25\nL127@P0: Acta Crystallographica Section E\nL128@P0: Structure Reports\nL129@P0: Online\nL130@P0-1: ISSN 1600-5368Smrcˇok, Lˇ ., Sla´dkovicˇova´, M., Langer, V., Wilson, C. C. & Koo´sˇ, M. (2006).\nL131@P1: Acta Cryst. B62, 912–918.\nL132@P1: Spek, A. L. (2003). J. Appl. Cryst. 36, 7–13.\nL133@P1: Wang, G.-M., Sun, Y.-Q. & Yang, G.-Y. (2004). J. Solid State Chem. 177, 4648–\nL134@P1: 4654.\nL135@P1: Yi, X.-Y., Liu, B., Jimenez-Aparicio, R., Urbanos, F. A., Gao, S., Xu, W., Chen,\nL136@P1: J.-S., Song, Y. & Zheng, L.-M. (2005). Inorg. Chem. 44, 4309–4314.\nL137@P1: inorganic compounds\nL138@P1-2: Acta Cryst. (2008). E64, i24–i25 Gainsford et al. \u0005 Na2[B4O5(OH)4]\u00018H2O i25supporting information\nL139@P2: Acta Cryst. (2008). E64, i24–i25 sup-1\nL140@P2: supporting information\nL141@P2: Acta Cryst. (2008). E64, i24–i25 [doi:10.1107/S1600536808010441]\nL142@P2: Redetermination of the borax structure from laboratory X-ray data at 145 K\nL143@P2: Graeme J. Gainsford, Tim Kemmitt and Caleb Higham\nL144@P2: S1. Comment\nL145@P2: The crystal structure of the title compound was previously studied by Morimoto (1956) using X-ray and later by Levy &\nL146@P2: Lisensky (1978, hereafter LL) using neutron diffraction data.\nL147@P2: There are 8 other compounds with free tetraborate anions [B4O5(OH)4]2- reported in the Cambridge Structural Database\nL148@P2: [C.S.D., version 5.29 with November 2007 updates (Allen, 2002)] with most containing protonated amine-based cations,\nL149@P2: e.g. DALQEN (Wang et al., 2004) and SIBDIR (Pan et al., 2007). The tetraborate anion in borax has 2-fold symmetry\nL150@P2: with the axis passing through O1 (Fig. 1) as is observed in five of the related structures. Both Na1 and Na2 cations are on\nL151@P2: special positions (centre of symmetry and 2-fold axis, respectively) so that they elegantly bind via shared water molecules\nL152@P2: in a typical zigzag cationic chain [Na(H2O)4/2(H2O)2/1] parallel to the c axis (e.g. DARNOA, Yi et al., 2005), as shown in\nL153@P2: Figure 2. As is found through a C.S.D. search of similar Na+/H2O cation chains, the Na–O distances to the bridging water\nL154@P2: molecules are longer than those to non-bridging water molecules, where the trans related Na–O distances belong to non\u0002bridging water molecules.\nL155@P2: The results of the present study and the LL model are essentially superimposable, but do reflect expected differences\nL156@P2: associated with the H atom positions: The systematic pairwise study (Allen, 1986) gave a difference for O–H (X-ray\nL157@P2: versus neutron) of -0.155 (10) Å, while a more recent study of levoglucosan (Smrčok et al., 2006) averaged at -0.016 (6)\nL158@P2: Å. The mean O—H distance here (0.843 (17) Å) is significantly shorter than for the neutron set (0.97 (1) Å). As the O···O\nL159@P2: distances involved in the hydrogen bonding are very similar for both studies (Table 1), the observed H···O distances are\nL160@P2: correspondingly longer here than in the LL model. We also note that average Na–O distances are marginally longer\nL161@P2: (0.006 (6) Å) and the B–O distances marginally shorter (-0.005 (2) Å) in the LL model, e.g. Na–O6, B1–O2 are 2.458 (3),\nL162@P2: 1.500 (2) Å compared with 2.4441 (6), 1.5075 (8) Å, respectively, in the present study. These latter differences are barely\nL163@P2: significant given that the neutron data set was collected at 296.5 K.\nL164@P2: Cell cohesion is provided by strong O—H···O hydrogen bonds of two types: (1) tetraborate anions \"head to tail\" link via\nL165@P2: the O5–H and O2 atoms (entry 1, Table 1) to form anionic chains as also seen in DALQEN (Wang et al., 2004); (2) the\nL166@P2: anionic and cationic chains crosslink through the water & tetraborate strong O–H···O hydrogen bond interactions (entries\nL167@P2: 2–10; see also Fig. 2 and diagrams in the LL study).\nL168@P2: S2. Experimental\nL169@P2: To a tetrahydrofuran (thf) solution (90 ml) of sodium tetrahydridoborate (0.31 g, 8.4 mmol) was added 0.5 g (4.2 mmol)\nL170@P2: of diaminomethane dihydrochloride. After 24 h, the solvent was removed and the remaining product dissolved in water.\nL171@P2: Methanol was added and the solution was left in a refrigerator. A small clump of colourless crystals of the title compound\nL172@P2-3: appeared after several days in the bottom of the flask.supporting information\nL173@P3: Acta Cryst. (2008). E64, i24–i25 sup-2\nL174@P3: S3. Refinement\nL175@P3: A total of 13 reflections (below 50°/2θ) were not collected. In the present re-determination the same atomic labels and\n"
}
